    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the full test suite is green (1 test run, 0 failures). The audit log line confirms the new behavior: `owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-LEV-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-11 membershipLevel=3`.\n\nHere's what I changed to implement the numeric `membershipLevel`:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` string field with a `membershipLevel` integer field (int32, min 1, max 3, readOnly). Since DTOs are generated from this spec at build time, `OwnerDto` now exposes `membershipLevel` instead of `membershipTier`.\n\n2. **`MembershipLevelResolver.java`** (new) \u2014 A small helper (mirroring the existing `LocalityResolver` pattern) computing the level: start at 1, `+1` when an email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3 (level 4 reserved for tenure).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Changed the derived mapping from `membershipTier` (the old GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE logic) to `membershipLevel`, computed via the resolver from the owner's stored `email` and `namesakeCount`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Updated the create audit line to record `membershipLevel=` (using the same resolver) in place of tier, and removed the now-stale javadoc referencing the removed GOLD tier.\n\nNote on one point of ambiguity: the spec's parenthetical said the create audit line \"recorded the tier,\" but in this codebase it actually logged only `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` \u2014 no tier. I honored the intent (\"must record the level instead\") by adding `membershipLevel` to that audit line; no existing test asserts the audit format, so this is safe.",
